{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nS1 proves one atomic update executor and S2 produces pure plans, but legacy batch dispatch still replays compound methods, conflates deferred and applied work, and has no endpoint-failure fuse and relies on ad-hoc nested per-call retries that cannot distinguish physical attempts from exhausted logical outcomes (the only breaker today is the blast-radius mass-mutation guard `classify.check_blast_radius`, which is a different concern). One ticket-local or permanent failure can distort batch behavior.\n\n## Outcome\nMake the typed coordinator authoritative for non-create plans: update, delete, probe, clean-label, repair-property, and conflict handling. Execute atomic desired postconditions in dependency order; after partial success, re-observe and plan only remaining drift. Produce exact ticket/pass outcomes and apply the approved endpoint/provider finite-pass fuse only to normalized exhausted logical failures.\n\n## Plan\n1. Add a coordinator that consumes immutable plans, enforces dependencies, and calls adapter atomic operations through S1s budget. Preserve successful earlier postconditions and never replay the whole compound mutation after a late failure.\n2. Normalize auto-healed, success, deferred, permanent, commit-unknown, exhausted transient, and skipped dispositions with ticket/endpoint/tenant/provider/global scope.\n3. Add a new endpoint-failure scoped fuse (none exists today). Endpoint scope opens after three consecutive eligible exhausted logical outcomes across at least two tickets; provider scope additionally requires the failing outcomes to span >=2 distinct endpoints of the same provider. A matching success resets consecutive state. The existing blast-radius mass-mutation guard (`classify.check_blast_radius`) is a distinct protection and is retained unchanged.\n4. Exclude physical attempts, recovered warnings, permanent failures, ticket-local 404, and opaque diagnostics from inferred fuse counters. Retain direct authoritative credential, configuration, quota, rate, issue, endpoint, tenant, and global behavior.\n5. When a fuse opens, defer remaining matching plans with reason/scope/retry metadata while independent scopes continue. Preserve exact applied/failed/deferred/skipped tallies and nonzero degraded-pass signaling.\n\n## Testing (RED → GREEN)\n- Coordinator tables cover dependency success/failure, partial compound update, idempotent already-gone delete, ticket-local 404 isolation, permanent 4xx, auth/quota/rate scopes, and mixed endpoints of one provider.\n- Fuse sequences assert threshold, two-ticket/two-distinct-endpoint requirements, success reset, exclusions, and independent-scope continuation.\n- Replay fixtures derived from historical GitHub Actions outcomes assert zero premature stop in the completed 1,200-run slice; raw warning/grep text is never classified as a terminal outcome.\n- Verified-fake Cloud/DC batches assert wire order, maximum calls, exact tallies, logs, and exit status.\n\n## Compatibility / rollback\nCut over by non-create operation family and never dual-send. Before legacy removal, an operation-family flag can select the old dispatcher. Rollback preserves successful Jira effects and resumes from re-observation. The intentional differences are three-call shared budgeting, explicit deferral, exact partial outcomes, and a new endpoint-failure scoped fuse (the blast-radius mass-mutation guard is retained).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] Non-create plans execute atomic postconditions once in dependency order; a late failure preserves earlier success and re-observes only remaining drift within the same logical budget.\n- [x] Ticket-local/permanent failures isolate the ticket, authoritative broad scopes stop only their declared scope, and independent tickets/endpoints continue.\n- [x] Endpoint fuse opens only after three consecutive eligible exhausted logical outcomes across two tickets; provider fuse additionally requires >=2 distinct endpoints of the same provider; matching success resets state.\n- [x] Physical attempts, recovered warnings, permanent failures, ticket-local 404s, and opaque diagnostics never increment inferred fuse counters.\n- [x] Remaining matching work is explicitly deferred with reason/scope/`retry_not_before`; applied, failed, deferred, skipped, and recovered tallies plus exit status are exact.\n- [x] Historical replay fixtures and verified-fake Cloud/DC batches show no premature termination of observed successful/partially successful work.\n\n## Fuse scope, eligibility & counter semantics (resolves E2/G6)\nGrounded entirely in existing `operation_outcome` types — no new \"family\" concept.\n- **Endpoint / provider.** *Endpoint* = one concrete provider instance the coordinator dispatches to (a single Jira base URL/site), the unit `FailureScope.endpoint` already denotes; *provider* = the vendor behind ≥1 endpoints (`FailureScope.provider`).\n- **Attribution.** `OperationOutcome` carries `logical_id` + authoritative `failure_scope` but no endpoint id, so the **coordinator** (the adapter's caller) tags each outcome with the `(provider, endpoint)` it dispatched to; inferred counters key off that identity, never off diagnostic text. A direct authoritative `failure_scope` of endpoint/tenant/provider/global still stops that scope immediately (step 4), independent of the counter.\n- **Eligible exhausted outcome** — keyed off **budget-exhaustion provenance, not the disposition value**. The coordinator drives every transient retry through the shared per-op `RetryBudget`; an adapter's first-attempt transient (`request_count=1`, `invocation_count=1`, `cumulative_sleep_ms=0`) is absorbed *inside* that retry loop and is never a pass outcome. An op becomes an eligible terminal outcome only when the shared budget itself caps it: `plan_next_retry` returns `action=\"exhausted\"` (MAX_INVOCATIONS reached) -> `exhausted_transient`, or `action=\"defer\"` (would exceed `MAX_CUMULATIVE_SLEEP_MS` before MAX_INVOCATIONS) -> terminal `retryable_deferred` carrying `retry_not_before` and a consumed allowance (`invocation_count>1` or `cumulative_sleep_ms` at cap) — the slow/rate-limited mode that never surfaces `exhausted_transient`. The mid-loop `decide_replay(old_conclusive, budget_remaining=True)` variant returns `replay=True` and is not a pass outcome. **Excluded**: `commit_unknown` (opaque/replay-forbidden), `recovered`, `already_satisfied`, `permanent_failure`, ticket-local 404, and any `retryable_deferred` the shared budget did NOT itself produce at its cap (e.g. a raw first-attempt adapter transient).\n- **`commit_unknown` disposition (not merely a counter exclusion).** An ambiguous/unverifiable write — an adapter that cannot confirm its mutation committed, or `decide_replay(observation=\"failed\"|\"inconclusive\")` — resolves to `Disposition.commit_unknown`, which the coordinator disposes as follows: (a) **ticket-scoped** — it isolates only its own ticket and, carrying no broad `failure_scope`, never stops sibling tickets/endpoints nor feeds the endpoint/provider fuse; (b) projected onto the **`deferred`** AC6 outcome bucket (`failure_policy._BUCKET_BY_DISPOSITION[commit_unknown] == \"deferred\"`), never `applied` or `failed`, so a possibly-applied-but-unconfirmed write is counted as neither success nor hard failure; (c) **replay-forbidden** for the remainder of the pass (`retry_budget.replay_safety_for(commit_unknown) -> ReplaySafety.forbidden`) — the coordinator does not re-issue the mutation, leaving the residual drift to be safely re-observed on the next `reconcile_once` pass rather than risking a double-apply.\n- **Counter durability/order.** Per-pass, in-memory, per-scope; zeroed each `reconcile_once(pass_id)` pass (no durable cross-pass state, consistent with S2's fresh Observation/version). \"Consecutive\" is per-scope over the coordinator's deterministic dependency-ordered execution; other scopes' interleaved outcomes neither increment nor reset a scope's counter (so independent scopes continue). A same-scope success (`applied`/`recovered`/`already_satisfied`) resets that scope to zero.\n- **Thresholds.** Endpoint opens after 3 consecutive eligible outcomes across ≥2 tickets on that endpoint within the pass; provider additionally requires ≥2 distinct endpoints of that provider. On open, remaining matching plans defer with reason/scope/`retry_not_before`; independent scopes keep running. A persistently-bad endpoint re-trips each pass that alone reaches threshold."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787686671664128001,"uuid":"861b768e-77d1-42a1-a3c7-fea63c37d6dc"}